WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.860
Plugins are one reason Moodle is flexible, and one reason Moodle administration requires discipline.

2
00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:08.920
An installed plugin is not just an extra feature.

3
00:00:09.020 --> 00:00:12.620
Once courses depend on it, the plugin becomes part of the service.

4
00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:23.120
It can affect upgrades, backups, restore behavior, privacy records, performance, accessibility, security review, support training, and user expectations.

5
00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:29.460
Administrators should treat plugins as governed dependencies rather than casual experiments.

6
00:00:29.560 --> 00:00:35.320
The question is not only whether a feature works today, but whether the organization can support it tomorrow.

7
00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:38.960
A plugin request should answer practical questions.

8
00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:40.740
What problem does it solve?

9
00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:42.600
Who owns the decision?

10
00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:46.940
Is it compatible with the deployed Moodle branch and the planned upgrade path?

11
00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:48.720
Is it actively maintained?

12
00:00:48.820 --> 00:00:50.900
Does it process personal data?

13
00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:56.600
Does it affect assessment, enrolment, authentication, reporting, or completion?

14
00:00:56.700 --> 00:00:58.780
What happens if it stops working?

15
00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:04.000
How will it be tested, documented, supported, monitored, and eventually removed?

16
00:01:04.280 --> 00:01:07.080
Integrations need the same care.

17
00:01:07.180 --> 00:01:21.500
Identity providers, student information systems, payment services, video platforms, plagiarism tools, content repositories, reporting platforms, and communication services all introduce sources of truth and failure modes.

18
00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:32.320
The administrator should know which system owns each data element, how synchronization is triggered, how errors appear, who receives alerts, and what learners experience during outages.

19
00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:36.760
Appearance and theme decisions are operational decisions too.

20
00:01:36.860 --> 00:01:45.820
Branding matters, but it should not outrank usability, accessibility, upgrade compatibility, mobile behavior, or support simplicity.

21
00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:52.480
Heavy theme customization can make future upgrades harder and can hide standard Moodle behaviors that support teams expect.

22
00:01:52.580 --> 00:01:58.900
A good visual design helps users orient themselves without turning every page into a local exception.

23
00:01:59.180 --> 00:02:03.660
Change control does not need to be theatrical, but it does need to be real.

24
00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:19.120
A material change should move through requirement, impact assessment, compatibility review, test environment, backup and rollback plan, approval, deployment, validation, documentation, and monitoring.

25
00:02:19.220 --> 00:02:24.500
The larger the change, the more evidence the administrator should collect before and after release.

26
00:02:24.780 --> 00:02:31.420
Managed hosting can reduce some server responsibilities, but it does not remove administrative responsibility.

27
00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:42.880
The organization still owns identity choices, role assignments, course governance, plugin decisions, communication expectations, privacy handling, and support workflows.

28
00:02:42.980 --> 00:02:52.580
The healthiest Moodle sites maintain a plugin and integration inventory that explains not only what is installed, but why it exists and what would break if it changed.

29
00:02:52.680 --> 00:03:00.040
That inventory becomes especially valuable when a release deadline, privacy review, or urgent security update arrives.
